Q: What is the difference between link wrapping and &#39;use client&#39; in Next.js? A: <p>TLDR: The <code>'use client'</code> directive is a feature of React while the <code>Link</code> component is a custom component of the NextJS framework. They're different unrelated things and that serve different unrelated purposes.</p>
<p>It’s been a minute since I’ve used Next, so if someone chimes in here or documents exist that contradict what I say then trust those other sources more. I’m also not familiar with that button component you’re using from what appears to be the <code>mantine</code> package, so depending on how they implement it, that could also make a difference.</p>
<p>If you're creating a component that needs client side features, such as <code>localstorage</code>, then it needs to be a client component (and needs the <code>'use client'</code> directive). Generally, if something doesn't have to be a client component then it probably shouldn't be (as @chhorm-ratha mentioned).</p>
<p>If you want a link for navigating within your NextJS web app then you use the <code>Link</code> component.</p>
<p><code>'use server'</code> really has little to no bearing on the use of <code>Link</code> and vice versa.</p>
<hr />
<p>The rest of this isn't really addressing your question, but more so addresses a possible misunderstanding of the <code>Link</code> and <code>a</code> (anchor) components in the context of the NextJS framework where they serve complimentary purposes to each other.</p>
<ul>
<li>The <code>Link</code> component is for route navigation <em>within</em> your NextJS web app. The <code>Link</code> component <em>can</em> be used to link to external web pages outside of your NextJS web app, but isn't what it's intended for nor should be used for.</li>
<li>The <code>a</code> (anchor) component is for navigation to web pages <em>outisde</em> your NextJS web app. Conversely, the <code>a</code> (anchor) component <em>can</em> be used to link to web pages within your NextJS web app, but shouldn't be used for that as that's the whole purpose of the <code>Link</code> component.</li>
</ul>
<p>In your first code snippet example</p>
<pre class="lang-jsx prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;Link href=&quot;/voice_monitor&quot; passHref legacyBehavior&gt;
  &lt;Button component=&quot;a&quot;&gt;Go to Voice Monitor&lt;/Button&gt;
&lt;/Link&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>You're wrapping a <code>Button</code> in a <code>Link</code> component and then setting the <code>component</code> prop of the <code>Button</code> component nested within the <code>Link</code> component to an <code>a</code> (anchor) element. Generally, most libraries that implement a <code>component</code> prop of a component makes it so that component actually gets rendered in the browser as the component passed to the <code>component</code> prop. So if you were to do something like:</p>
<pre class="lang-jsx prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;Button component=&quot;a&quot;&gt;Go to Voice Monitor&lt;/Button&gt;
&lt;Button component={Link} href=&quot;/voice_monitor&quot;&gt;Go to Voice Monitor&lt;/Button&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>The actual rendered html for that Button component would be:</p>
<pre class="lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>// note the missing href attribute since it wasn't set it in the button component above
&lt;a&gt;Go to Voice Monitor&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;Link href=&quot;/voice_monitor&quot;&gt;Go to Voice Monitor&lt;/Link&gt;
// technically, I believe Link components actually get rendered as anchor elements so the
// line directly above this isn't actually 100% correct to what gets rendered in the browser
</code></pre>
<p>So you're essentially wrapping an <em>anchor</em> element in a <code>Link</code> component which doesn't really make sense and is probably not what you're intending or wanting to do as their functionality overlaps.</p>
<p>Edit: I want to point out that above, saying the <code>'use client'</code> directive is for a &quot;component that needs client side features...&quot; is true, but doesn't actually explain anything and is a bit of a blanket statement doing a lot of heavy lifting. There's a good chance you've probably already read them, but the <a href="https://react.dev/reference/rsc/use-client" rel="nofollow noreferrer">React docs</a> and <a href="https://nextjs.org/docs/app/api-reference/directives/use-client" rel="nofollow noreferrer">NextJS docs</a> on the <code>'use client'</code> directive are rather good as are the <a href="https://nextjs.org/docs/app/api-reference/components/link" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Link component docs</a>. Definitely worth a read if you haven't checked them out already and despite them being relatively good, even after reading them, chances are you'll probably have to re-read them and try implementing them several times until it fully clicks.</p>
